<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Addizione Erculea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1492</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Herculean Addition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Addizione Erculea was a planned extension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> begun in 1492 for Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este">Ercole I d'Este</a> and designed by the architect Biagio Rossetti. It roughly doubled the city's area with broad straight streets, new walls and palaces such as the Palazzo dei Diamanti, and is often called the first modern city plan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It is part of Ferrara's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage listing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea.jpg" alt="Addizione Erculea" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pallares1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferrara_diamanti3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
